# What is pinned in the dock, and in what order (#263).
#
# The user report was "assistant doesn't open". Nothing was wrong with
# the Assistant: its .desktop, its D-Bus .service and its Super+C
# binding were all present and working. It was simply not reachable —
# not in the dock, and Show Apps was broken (#262), so the app grid
# fallback was gone too. Two harmless-looking defaults produced a
# missing feature.
#
# THE ORDER IS AN ARGUMENT, NOT AN APPEND:
#
#   1. Assistant. Lisa is an AI-native OS; this is the surface that
#      makes it one. Pinning it fourth, or not at all, means the
#      defining feature is the one thing a first-boot user cannot see —
#      and a person decides what a machine is from what is in front of
#      them, not from what a shortcut would have done.
#   2. Files. Where nearly every other task starts, and the only icon
#      the reference device's owner had kept after rearranging their
#      own dock — evidence about reach beats taste about it. It was
#      also missing from the pin list entirely, so pinning anything at
#      all would have taken Files away from them.
#   3. Surfer, 4. Mail. The two apps a person lives in all day, browser
#      before mail because the browser is opened for anything and mail
#      is opened for mail.
#   5. Preview, 6. Ledger. Reached mostly from a file or a link, so
#      they sit after the four that are reached from the dock itself.
#   7. Console, last: the one a person opens deliberately, and the one
#      whose presence in the first four positions would say something
#      untrue about who this OS is for.
#
# WHY HERE AS WELL AS IN THE PACKAGE. dconf is a settings backend, so
# this file reaches whichever shell the image runs, including a
# private-prefix Lisa Desktop that would never read a gschema override
# (ADR-0038). The package's 10_lisa-shell.gschema.override carries the
# identical list for Track L, which ships no dconf at all;
# os/repo-tools/check-dock.py fails the lint gate if the two drift.
#
# WHAT THIS CANNOT DO. It is a default, and a default sits underneath
# the user's own dconf. GNOME Shell writes favorite-apps whenever
# anything is pinned or unpinned, so a machine that has already been
# used has a value of its own and keeps it. This fixes first boot; it
# does not reach back into an existing profile, and pretending
# otherwise is how "shipped" comes to mean something different from
# "present on the device".
#
# Defaults, not locks: no locks/ directory ships, so a user's own dock
# arrangement is theirs and survives every update.
[org/gnome/shell]
favorite-apps=['app.lisaos.Assistant.desktop', 'org.gnome.Nautilus.desktop', 'app.lisaos.Surfer.desktop', 'app.lisaos.Mail.desktop', 'app.lisaos.Preview.desktop', 'app.lisaos.LedgerApp.desktop', 'org.gnome.Console.desktop']
